Disclaimer

This directory contains software for generating and manipulating interpolation
weights for use with the Interpolation On the Fly (IOF) option in NEMO
v3 onwards.  The utilities rely heavily on the Spherical Coordinate Remapping
and Interpolation Package (SCRIP) which is freely available from Los Alamos
(see copyright file). We make no claim to authorship of those parts of the
SCRIP package we have used and included with this distribution.

Introduction

The IOF option enables forced ocean runs without the need to provide surface
boundary data on the nemo grid. Atmospheric data are kept on their original
grid(s) which typically have a coarser resolution than the ocean grid. IOF
supports multiple source (atmospheric) grids and 3 interpolation methods
(bilinear, bicubic and distwgt). Different combinations of source grid and method
can be selected for each variable. IOF is also compatible with AGRIF; allowing
AGRIF nests to be used without the need to provide separate forcing data
(only a separate set of weights fields). Operational details are provided
in the SBC chapter of the NEMO reference manual.

Please note that the methods described do accept masked values.
We however sugest users to provide unmasked data after filling masked points
with appropriate oceanic values on the source grid.
This is particularly important for atmospheric variables which experience
step changes at the land-sea boundary. For these, care must be taken to
avoidcontaminating oceanic values.

The software is divided into 3 main programs:

scripgrid.exe (scripgrid.F90) which creates two grid description files suitable for
input to the scrip package.
Output file can also be used as grid description file in cdo interpolation functions

scrip.exe (scrip.F90) which takes these descriptions and generates the required
weights in the standard SCRIP format. This program is a version of the SCRIP
program: scrip.f which has been modified to accept the name of its namelist
file on the command line.
Note that, scrip.exe is the equivalent of cdo genbil, genbic or gendis

scripshape.exe (scripshape.F90) which takes the output from scrip and rearranges
the fields into a series of 2D fields more suited for input into NEMO using
the iom_get routine.

Installation is by use of the maketools script.

  cd tools
  ./maketools -help

and, for example:

  ./maketools -m gfortran_linux -n WEIGHTS


Example Use
-----------

  cd tools/WEIGHTS
  ./scripgrid.exe  namelist_bilin    (creates data_scrip_grid.nc and nemo_scrip_grid.nc
				      with the namelist example given bellow)
  ./scrip.exe      namelist_bilin    (creates data_nemo_remap_bilin.nc)
				      with the namelist example given bellow)
  ./scripshape.exe namelist_bilin    (creates weights_bilin.nc)
  				      with the namelist example given bellow)

Only the final output file is required for use with NEMO but the intermediate

Note that, following this example, the call to scrip.exe could be replaced by

  cdo genbil,nemo_scrip_grid.nc snow_1m_TRP_1958.nc data_nemo_remap_bilin.nc


Controls
--------

The SCRIP derived utilities use SCRIP-style namelists to control their operation.
In the example above the same named file is used for both but within that
file are separate namelists blocks used by each of the 3 executable

grid_inputs is used by scripgrid.exe: 

&grid_inputs
    grid1_grid_in = 'snow_1m_TRP_1958.nc'
      grid1_lon = 'lon'
      grid1_lat = 'lat'
      grid1_mask = 'none'
      grid1_mask_value = 10.
    grid1_file = 'data_scrip_grid.nc'
    !
    grid2_grid_in = 'domain_cfg.nc'
      grid2_lon = 'glamt'
      grid2_lat = 'gphit'
      grid2_mask = 'top_level'
      grid2_mask_value = 0.
    grid2_file = 'nemo_scrip_grid.nc'
/

remap_inputs is used by scrip.exe: 

&remap_inputs
    num_maps = 1
    grid1_file = 'data_scrip_grid.nc'
    grid2_file = 'nemo_scrip_grid.nc'
    interp_file1 = 'data_nemo_remap_bilin.nc'
    interp_file2 = 'nemo_data_remap_bilin.nc'
    map1_name = 'data to nemo bilin Mapping'
    map2_name = 'nemo to data bilin Mapping'
    map_method = 'bilinear'
/

shape_inputs is used by scripshape.exe: 

&shape_inputs
    interp_file = 'data_nemo_remap_bilin.nc'
    output_file = 'weights_bilin.nc'
    ew_wrap     = 0
/

scripgrid.exe
-------------

scripgrid accesses the named files in order to produce the grid centre and
corner locations required by scrip. The corner locations are fake (set to -9999.)
as the scrip libary requires them but don't use them for bilinear, bicubic
and distance-weighted interpolation.
grid1_grid_in and grid2_grid_in must exist and must contain at least the
longitude and latitude coordinate variables, which can be 1 or 2D arrays.

If the mask definition is unchanged between the different mapping methods
(i.e. bilinear, distwgt or bicubic), only one set of scrip grid files needs
to be produced for each pair of source and destination grids.

 - grid1_grid_in, grid2_grid_in: names of grid1 and grid2 input files
 
 - grid1_lon, grid2_lon: names of grid1 and grid2 longitude variables
 
 - grid1_lat, grid2_lat: names of grid1 and grid2 latitude variables
 
 - grid1_mask, grid2_mask: names of grid1 and grid2 mask variables
 
 - grid1_mask_value, grid1_mask_value: value of grid1 and grid2 masked points
 
 - grid1_file, grid2_file: names of grid1 and grid2 output in scrip format

scrip.exe
---------

scrip takes the output files from scripgrid and creates a file of weights
to use to interpolate between the two grids described. The example given
only requests 1 map (from data to nemo); setting num_maps=2 will provide
the reverse weights if required.
 
 - num_maps: 1 or 2 depending on whether the reverse transformation is required 

 - grid1_file, grid2_file: names of input scrip grid files (output of scripgrid)

 - interp_file1, interp_file2: names of the output scrip interp_file

 - map1_name, map2_name: the map_name variables are just descriptive

 - map_method: name of the interpolation method: 'bilinear', 'bicubic' or 'distwgt'

The conservative mapping schemes cannot be used as the corner definition
cannot properly be done for the first column/row based on the input grid file.
A proper definition requires to be done on-line when running NEMO.

scripshape.exe
--------------

scripshape takes the output from scrip (e.g. data_nemo_bilin.nc ) and
rearranges the source indices and associated weights into sets of 2D
fields each spanning the nemo grid.
For a bilinear mapping, the weights file required by the IOF option
includes the following fields:

  double src01(lat, lon) ;   double wgt01(lat, lon) ;
  double src02(lat, lon) ;   double wgt02(lat, lon) ;
  double src03(lat, lon) ;   double wgt03(lat, lon) ;
  double src04(lat, lon) ;   double wgt04(lat, lon) ;

For a distance-weighted mapping with n neighbourgs, the required fields are:

  double src01(lat, lon) ;   double wgt01(lat, lon) ;
  ...
  double src0n(lat, lon) ;   double wgt0n(lat, lon) ;

For a bicubic mapping the required fields are:

  double src01(lat, lon) ;   double wgt01(lat, lon) ;
  double src02(lat, lon) ;   double wgt02(lat, lon) ;
  double src03(lat, lon) ;   double wgt03(lat, lon) ;
  double src04(lat, lon) ;   double wgt04(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt05(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt06(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt07(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt08(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt09(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt10(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt11(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt12(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt13(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt14(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt15(lat, lon) ;
                             double wgt16(lat, lon) ;

Only for the bicubic interpolation, this program also adds an attribute to
the weights file for use by the fld_interp routine in fldread.F90. This
tells the model about the east-west cyclicity of the source grid which can
be used when calculating gradient values.
The value needs to be supplied in the scripshape namelist via the
variable ew_wrap. It should have one of the following values:
  n<0: input grid is not cyclic;
  n=0: input grid is cyclic but with no overlapping columns
  n>0: input grid is cyclic but with n  columns that overlap

scripinterp.exe
---------------

Take data on an input grid and interpolate to the nemo grid using the weights
calculated by the scrip program.
Method
Two namelists are used for configuration, eg

&interp_inputs
    input_file = "../data/wsx_av.nc"
    interp_file = "data_nemo_bilin.nc"
    input_name = "wsx"
    input_start = 1,1,1,1
    input_stride = 1,1,1,1
    input_stop = 0,0,0,1
    input_vars = 'time'
/

&interp_outputs
    output_file = "taux_1m.nc"
    output_mode = "create"
    output_dims = 'x', 'y', 'time_counter'
    output_scaling = "sozotaux|1.0", "time_counter|86400.0"
    output_name = 'sozotaux'
    output_lon = 'x'
    output_lat = 'y'
    output_vars = 'time_counter'
    output_attributes = 'time_counter|units|seconds since 1995-00-00 00:00:00',
                        'time_counter|calendar|noleap',
                        'sozotaux|units|N/m2'
/

This program just multiplies by weights and sums over each contributing point,
and then formats the output correctly for the model.

